"""Cyber Explorers guided investigation manuscript; independent editorial review pending."""
COURSE = {
  "key": "explorers-investigate-v1",
  "title": "Investigating Suspicious Messages & Activity",
  "academy_id": "secondary",
  "pathway": "secondary",
  "stage": "investigate",
  "revision": "v1",
  "description": "Cyber Explorers stage 4: eight long-form investigations of messages, sign-in events, device incidents, evidence and safe escalation.",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "position": 1,
      "title": "Recording exactly what happened",
      "content": "**Your first job is to make a useful record.** By the end of this lesson you should be able to describe a suspicious event, keep the order of your own actions clear and tell a trusted helper what remains unknown. Earlier courses introduced safer account and browsing habits. Here you will practise investigating a small fictional case using evidence already supplied. You do not need to search a real person's account, contact a suspicious sender or use technical investigation software.\n\nWorked example: At 16:20, Amaka sees a message in a class group: “The art-club gallery closes tonight. Use this new upload page.” At 16:24 she opens the page and sees a request to sign in. She closes it without typing anything. At 16:30 a friend says the page is a scam. An inaccurate report says, “At 16:20 a hacker stole Amaka's password.” A useful report says what was seen and what Amaka did. Neither the friend's warning nor the sign-in request proves that a password was stolen.\n\n**Use a diary with five fields.** For each entry record the time, observation, action, result and question. An observation is what you noticed, such as the words on a page. An action is what you did, such as closing it. A result is what happened afterward. A question is something not yet established, such as whether the club authorised that page. Keeping these separate prevents a worried interpretation from turning into a supposed fact. Approximate times are acceptable if labelled; do not invent exact minutes to make the diary look complete.\n\n**A worked diary entry.** “16:24, approximate: page asked for a school sign-in; I closed the page; I had not typed or submitted credentials; I have not confirmed whether the club issued the request.” This entry answers several practical questions without diagnosing the page. It is more helpful than “I clicked something bad.” If Amaka is unsure whether the browser filled a field automatically, she should say so. The helper can then ask a focused follow-up instead of relying on a false assurance that nothing was entered.\n\n**Distinguish when things happened.** The time printed beside a message can differ from when someone read it. A screenshot captured at 17:00 may show a message sent earlier. Write which time you mean. In this course's cards, all stated times are from the same fictional local clock unless a card says otherwise. Real records may have different clocks. A beginner can still write “noticed after school, exact time unknown” without pretending to resolve a technical timing issue. Accuracy includes admitting the limits of memory.\n\n**Keep later information as a new entry.** At 17:05 the known club organiser confirms through the school office that the club did not issue a new upload page. Add that confirmation and its source. Do not rewrite the first entry to say Amaka already knew the page was unauthorised. The difference matters: it shows which decision was made before verification and which conclusion followed it. It also avoids blaming a learner for not knowing information that arrived later.\n\n**Try the case.** Create diary entries for the 16:20 message, 16:24 page visit, 16:30 friend's warning and 17:05 office confirmation. Then assess three draft claims: the class group received an upload request; Amaka supplied her password; the club did not authorise the new page. Mark each supported, unsupported or needing clarification. Your diary should use fictional labels and should not include any real password, private conversation or live suspicious address.\n\n**Explained answer.** The upload request is supported by the first observation. Password disclosure is unsupported by the supplied case, which says Amaka typed nothing; uncertainty about automatic entry would need its own explicit note if introduced. Lack of club authorisation is supported by the later independently reached office confirmation. The friend's warning is a report to consider, not evidence identifying who operated the page. A suitable next step is a factual report to the school's established support route, with no need to visit the page again.\n\n**Reflection.** Give your diary to someone who has not read the story. Can they tell what happened before the warning and what was learned afterward? Correct missing facts or unclear wording. A useful record helps another person choose the next safe action; it does not become better by sounding more dramatic."
    },
    {
      "position": 2,
      "title": "Separating observations from assumptions",
      "content": "**A plausible story is still a story to test.** This lesson develops three skills: recognising an assumption inside a report, comparing two explanations and choosing a safe check that could separate them. An observation describes something visible or recorded. An assumption fills a gap. Assumptions are not automatically wrong, but they should not be presented as facts. The aim is to avoid an accusation or unnecessary change while still responding promptly when there is a real exposure.\n\nWorked example: A class gallery publishes a student's drawing at midnight. The student says, “Someone must have logged into my account while I was asleep.” The supplied evidence cards say that the gallery displays “published 00:00,” the drawing was uploaded by the student that afternoon, and the platform offers a scheduled-publication feature. Those cards do not establish who configured publication. They do show why midnight alone cannot identify another account user. Something unusual can have an ordinary explanation.\n\n**Compare explanations that predict different evidence.** Explanation one is a scheduled post selected earlier by the student. It predicts a matching scheduled item in the student's own activity history or a remembered scheduling choice. Explanation two is an unrecognised account action. It predicts an unfamiliar change or session that requires further review. Do not write “normal event” and “bad event” as your two explanations; those labels do not tell you what to check. The useful question is who or what caused this particular publication under the platform's actual features.\n\n**Choose a check within the learner's boundary.** In the fictional case, the student can review their own publication history through the genuine gallery service, with a trusted adult if needed. They should not ask a classmate to log in, give anyone their code or try accessing another student's history. If the gallery lacks a history view, its authorised support team may be able to help. An unavailable record is a limitation to report, not permission to investigate other people's accounts. You can seek help without first proving the most worrying theory.\n\n**Update the view with new evidence.** Card four says, “Scheduled for midnight; created at 15:42 by this account.” Card five says the student remembers choosing midnight because they thought the gallery required it. These facts strengthen the scheduling explanation, but the wording “by this account” does not physically identify a person. For this small learning case, the student's recollection and matching record explain the reported event well enough to correct the midnight-login claim. Nothing in the cards supports identifying a classmate as responsible.\n\n**Avoid two common reasoning errors.** First, several friends repeating the same rumour are not several independent observations. Ask what each person actually saw. Second, not finding a record is not always proof that the event never happened. The feature might not record it or the relevant history may no longer be available. Explain the specific gap instead of using it to claim either complete safety or definite compromise. Good reasoning keeps the confidence of the statement matched to the evidence.\n\n**Practice with a changed case.** The controller now removes cards four and five and provides a different pair: the student cannot access publication history, and an unrecognised recovery address appears in account settings. Write one sentence about what is confirmed, one about what remains uncertain and one safe next action. Decide whether the student should wait until they know who changed the address before using official account support.\n\n**Explained answer.** The account settings show an unrecognised recovery address, while the cause of the midnight publication remains unconfirmed. The learner should promptly use the provider's genuine security or recovery process, with trusted support as appropriate, without waiting to identify an operator. That action responds to the access concern; it does not prove the publication was malicious. In the earlier scheduling version, a public accusation would be unjustified. In the changed version, uncertainty is not a reason to ignore a concrete account-control concern.\n\n**Reflection.** Rewrite “Someone hacked me because the post appeared at midnight” using only what the original three cards establish. A useful answer names the publication time and says its cause is being checked. Explain which later card changes that interpretation most and why. This is the investigation skill: change your view because of evidence, not because one story feels more frightening."
    },
    {
      "position": 3,
      "title": "Tracing a message to an independent source",
      "content": "**Trace the claim, not just the logo.** By the end of this lesson you should be able to identify which organisation can answer a question, distinguish several copies of one message from separate confirmation and record what an official reply actually settles. Earlier lessons taught the habit of opening a known service independently. Here you will use that habit to build a source trail: where each statement came from and which statement depends on another. A source trail can be drawn on paper without opening any suspicious link.\n\nWorked example: A forwarded message says that the school's Saturday science visit now leaves at 06:30 instead of 08:00. It contains a screenshot, a new telephone number and a request to send a transport fee to a named account. Three classmates forward it. The school's saved trip page still says 08:00, but it was last updated on Tuesday. The visit is on Saturday, and a genuine late change is possible. The investigator's task is to establish the current departure instruction and whether the payment request is authorised.\n\n**Split a bundled message into claims.** The departure time and payment instruction are separate claims. A genuine time change would not automatically authenticate a new payment destination. Write each claim and the accountable source beside it. The school trip office can confirm its schedule and authorised payment arrangements. A classmate may confirm what they received, but cannot establish the school's current instruction merely by forwarding the same picture. This helps you ask a focused question instead of accepting or rejecting the entire message at once.\n\n**Map the source trail.** In the fictional evidence pack, classmates A, B and C all received their screenshot from group D. The screenshot's claimed office reply also came from group D. Draw four arrows back to that common origin. Four copies do not create four independent confirmations. In contrast, a known school office number obtained from the existing school directory is a separate route. Do not use the new number in the disputed message as the only way to confirm that same message.\n\n**Resolve a stale official source carefully.** The saved school page is genuine, yet it might not be current. Its age should prompt clarification, not an automatic claim that the new message is false. In card five, the trip office reached through the established directory confirms a 07:00 departure and says there is no extra fee. Ask the office to publish the correction through its usual channel. The source trail now contains stronger evidence about the school's instruction, and it also reveals that neither circulating departure time was current.\n\n**Write a precise correction.** A useful class update says that the trip office has confirmed 07:00 and no additional fee, and that learners should consult the school's recognised announcement route. It should not claim that every person forwarding the screenshot was trying to steal money. The office's reply establishes its own instruction; it does not identify who created the picture or why. Do not recirculate the payment account or suspicious destination as part of a broad warning when learners do not need them to act.\n\n**Your exercise.** Produce a two-row claim table for the departure time and fee. For each, show the original evidence, its weakness, the independent check and the result. Then answer a harder variant: the office cannot be reached before the school day ends, and the website remains unchanged. Decide whether “not verified” means “proved fake,” whether to pay and who should receive the unresolved question. Keep your response suitable for a younger student.\n\n**Explained answer.** In the completed case, the established office route contradicts both the forwarded time and fee instruction, while the school page needs updating. In the unreachable-office variant, the new demand remains unverified rather than proved fraudulent. Do not send an unexpected fee to an unconfirmed destination. Seek the known trip organiser or a trusted adult through an established school route, explaining the deadline and conflicting information. The legitimate goal is reaching the visit safely, not exposing the student to risk to solve the sender's identity.\n\n**Reflection.** Explain why an official source can be genuine but outdated, and why a copied official-looking reply is not the same as reaching the office yourself. A strong source trail shows which exact question was resolved and preserves any conflict that still needs an accountable answer."
    },
    {
      "position": 4,
      "title": "Investigating unfamiliar sign-ins",
      "content": "**Interpret a sign-in record before naming its meaning.** This lesson helps you distinguish an unsuccessful attempt, an active session and a change made inside an account. A session is a period of account access through a browser, app or service. Different providers display different details, and some show several sessions from the same device. You will reason from a fictional security page, not inspect a classmate's account or assume that every service uses identical labels.\n\nWorked example: The supplied page belongs to a fictional learner, Timi. Card A shows “Password attempt unsuccessful, 18:05.” Card B shows “Browser session active, first seen 14:10, last activity 18:07.” Card C shows “Recovery contact changed, 18:08.” Timi used a library computer at 14:10 but has been at home since 17:00. The approximate location beside card B names a nearby city. The task is to determine what each card establishes and which question should be taken to trusted support.\n\n**Read unsuccessful attempts narrowly.** Card A does not show that the attempted password was accepted. It may still justify attention, particularly if repeated, but it does not prove that messages were read. Avoid turning “attempt unsuccessful” into “account opened.” Also avoid assuming that this one failed attempt describes every route into the account. The active session in card B is separate evidence. An earlier valid session can remain relevant even when a later password attempt fails.\n\n**Do not treat activity time as proof of a person.** Timi's session began when they used the library computer. Later activity might reflect continued interaction or background communication, depending on the fictional provider's definition. The location is an estimate based on network information, not a precise identification of who used the keyboard. To decide whether a session should remain open, the learner needs to recognise the device and their intended use, not find the physical address of a suspected stranger. Searching for someone based on a city label is outside this exercise.\n\n**Look for a meaningful account change.** Card C needs clarification because recovery details can affect future access. In card D, Timi says they did not request that change. A genuine service record plus the owner's denial is a concrete reason to use official account-security support promptly. It does not identify who operated the session or establish whether card A caused anything. The learner can report the unexplained change and secure access through the provider's supported process without resolving every link first.\n\n**Keep response and proof separate.** If the provider offers remote sign-out, Timi can use it for the recognised library session they intended to close. If access or recovery details are uncertain, follow the provider's genuine recovery guidance. A password change and session sign-out may have different effects across services. After an action, record the displayed result, such as the session marked signed out, while recognising that a student may not be able to independently verify every internal access path. A trusted helper explains instructions without collecting Timi's password or private codes.\n\n**Exercise: assess the cards.** Make a three-row table naming what A, B and C establish, what they do not establish and the next safe question. Then review a calmer variant: there is no card C, Timi remembers the library session and the security page explains that recent activity can include automatic syncing. Would you say someone definitely used the account after Timi left? What protective action remains sensible for a session on a shared computer?\n\n**Explained answer.** A establishes one unsuccessful attempt. B establishes a displayed active session associated with the earlier library use, with later communication whose exact cause is not resolved. C, together with D, establishes an unrecognised recovery change that should be escalated promptly. In the calmer variant, background activity is a plausible explanation, so later human use is unproved. Signing out of a shared-device session the learner no longer needs remains sensible. The record can state the action without claiming a compromise occurred.\n\n**Reflection.** Explain why “I recognised the device” and “I intended to leave the account accessible there” are different questions. Your conclusion should connect the observed event to an appropriate action and leave human attribution unresolved unless there is dependable evidence for it."
    },
    {
      "position": 5,
      "title": "Assessing a suspicious download",
      "content": "**Establish the file's state before diagnosing the device.** In this lesson you will distinguish an offered download, a saved file and an opened or run file, then write a report that helps trusted support choose a proportionate response. Earlier study covered trusted software and permissions. This investigation focuses on what happened in one case. A familiar file name is not proof of a trustworthy origin, and the words “virus found” on a webpage are not automatically a finding from the device's real security system.\n\nWorked example: A message offers a free art-toolkit archive for the school club. It says, “Download this now, switch off protection if blocked, and open the installer.” Three fictional learners report different experiences. Amina only saw the message. Bayo saved a file but says he did not open it. Chidi opened the downloaded item and saw a permission request; he is unsure whether he accepted it. All three accounts need accurate recording, but they do not describe the same exposure.\n\n**Branch one: offered but not saved.** Amina should avoid the file and verify the club's resource through its known organiser or portal. Her report can include the offer's source, the request to disable protection and the fact that no download was made. There is no need to obtain the archive as evidence. An urgent or suspicious instruction justifies avoiding the interaction, but it does not prove what the unseen file contains. The learning task is a safe decision, not identifying malware by guessing from a message.\n\n**Branch two: saved, not knowingly opened.** Bayo records the source, approximate download time and what he remembers doing. On a school device, he uses the institution's reporting process and follows trusted support instructions. He must not open the file to make the report more certain, forward it to friends for testing or turn off protection. File presence alone does not establish execution. Equally, if he is unsure what the browser or another application did, write that uncertainty rather than promising that nothing could have happened.\n\n**Branch three: opened or possibly installed.** Chidi should stop interacting with the unknown item and promptly tell the responsible device owner or trusted support what was opened and what permission appeared. Support can determine appropriate containment for that device and service. The learner should not improvise an investigation by repeatedly running the file or deleting system components. If sensitive account activity or payment information may also be involved, mention the exposure without sending passwords. A school-managed computer and a personal phone may require different provider-supported actions.\n\n**Distinguish a webpage claim from a security alert.** A later exercise card shows a browser page claiming it has detected hundreds of infections and offering a paid cleaner. No independent device-security finding is supplied. The student should avoid the offered installation and use a known device-support route if concerned. Do not press buttons inside the page merely because they look like system controls. The claim can be reported as webpage content. Its intimidating appearance is not a technical examination of the device.\n\n**Practice the decision record.** For Amina, Bayo and Chidi, write four fields: known action, uncertainty, next responsible helper and information that helper needs. Add one sentence explaining why the instruction to disable protection increases concern. Then reject or correct these claims: all three devices are confirmed infected; Bayo should run the file to see whether it opens; Chidi must identify the sender before seeking help. Use only the supplied evidence rather than inventing a scan result.\n\n**Explained answer.** No confirmed infection result is given for any learner. Amina can report the offer without downloading it. Bayo can seek device-owner guidance based on the saved-file state. Chidi's possible execution and unclear permission decision make prompt support especially important. Running an unknown item would create new risk instead of safely resolving the original uncertainty. Sender identity is not a prerequisite for reporting possible exposure. The request to disable protection attempts to remove a safeguard, but does not alone identify the file's contents.\n\n**Reflection.** Explain how one word in a report, such as changing “downloaded” to “installed,” could lead a helper toward the wrong response. Finish with a calm support note for Chidi that states what happened, what is uncertain and what assistance is requested without making a diagnosis the evidence cannot support."
    },
    {
      "position": 6,
      "title": "Preserving useful evidence safely",
      "content": "**Useful evidence answers a question without exposing everyone around it.** This lesson develops the ability to choose relevant information, label changes to a copy and prepare different versions for different audiences. You do not need to collect a large evidence file. A short factual report may be enough for the responsible helper to ask the next question. Screenshots and messages can contain private details, so preserve and share them only through the appropriate process. This exercise uses a completely fictional message card.\n\nWorked example: A class chat contains a false-looking transport-fee demand. The screenshot also shows a friend's telephone number, an unrelated medical absence message and a clickable payment destination. The class moderator wants to post it publicly as a warning. The school support desk needs enough context to identify the reported demand. Those are different purposes. Publishing the whole image would reveal unrelated information and might encourage people to visit the disputed destination.\n\n**Choose the minimum that helps.** For a private report, useful details may include the service or group, when the demand appeared, its claimed purpose and whether anyone acted. Use the school's established reporting route to ask how any original should be submitted. Do not gather classmates' payment histories or passwords to strengthen the report. If the original contains sensitive material, do not place it in an open class folder while waiting for help. Ask a trusted adult or authorised staff member about secure handling when needed.\n\n**Make a teaching copy honestly.** A copy used in class can replace names with fictional labels and remove unrelated messages and active destinations. Label it “redacted discussion copy” and record what was removed. Redaction means removing information from the delivered copy, not adding a see-through shape or leaving the details elsewhere in the file. Use an approved method with a trusted helper and check the exported result. If you cannot reliably remove the information, write a text summary instead. You do not need an image to communicate the safe action.\n\n**Do not improve the evidence by changing the story.** Cropping can hide a reply that changes the meaning. Adding an arrow can help explain a feature, but it must be distinguishable from the original message. Never alter a time or replace the sender's wording to make a suspicion look stronger. Keep your own description separate from the observed content. Where an authorised team retains an original, a later reviewer can use that controlled context instead of relying only on a classroom copy.\n\n**Track handling in plain language.** A simple note might say: “Fictional card E2 supplied by tutor; discussion copy made at 12:00; names and unrelated lines removed; original exercise card unchanged.” A real support report would identify the actual source and approximate capture time without publishing unnecessary device or account details. Avoid calling this a formal forensic examination; you are practising clear handling and safe communication. Follow the responsible organisation's retention instructions rather than keeping every real incident image forever.\n\n**Two audience exercise.** From the supplied transport-fee case, write a private support note and a public class advisory. The private note should ask whether the school authorised the demand and identify what the reporter did. The public advisory should give classmates a safe action without exposing the unrelated conversation or reproducing a payment destination. Then inspect a proposed crop that removes the medical message but also cuts away an office reply saying “This demand is not ours.” Decide whether that crop is adequate on its own.\n\n**Explained answer.** A private note could state the approximate time and claimed fee, say that the learner did not pay and ask staff to confirm authorisation through the school's established process. A public advisory can advise checking the school office before acting on unexpected transport-fee requests. It need not identify classmates. The proposed crop omits relevant confirmation and could mislead a reader about what remains uncertain. A text summary that accurately includes the office's confirmed position may be better than that cropped image. The original should be handled only as the responsible team authorises.\n\n**Reflection.** Name one detail investigators might need that classmates do not, and one detail neither audience needs for this question. Explain how a smaller, well-labelled report can be more useful than a large unfiltered screenshot. Safe evidence handling protects both the investigation and the people whose information happened to appear beside the event."
    },
    {
      "position": 7,
      "title": "Escalating to trusted support",
      "content": "**A good escalation reaches someone able to act.** By the end of this lesson you should be able to choose the responsible helper, make a focused request and respond when the first channel is unavailable. Escalation is not passing blame or admitting failure. A learner can recognise a concern but lack the permissions, knowledge or authority needed to resolve it. The next step depends on what harm could continue, rather than whether the report sounds technically impressive.\n\nWorked example: A fictional learner, Sade, finds messages sent from her school account that she did not write. She can still open the genuine portal, but the displayed support contact is closed for the day. A new message offers “emergency school support” through a different chat number and asks for a sign-in code. Sade also has a known class teacher and an established school safeguarding contact. The challenge is to seek useful help without giving the new sender control of the account.\n\n**Match the problem to responsibility.** School technology staff can act on school-managed accounts. A payment provider can handle a disputed transfer through its genuine support process. A trusted adult or safeguarding professional can help when a student faces threats or personal-safety concerns. These roles can cooperate, but they are not interchangeable. A teacher might help locate the bank's real contact route, yet cannot reverse a payment by receiving a student's banking password. An unexpected sender's claimed title does not establish a legitimate support role.\n\n**State the request clearly.** A useful note has the affected service, observed event, approximate time, actions already taken and the help requested. For Sade, the request is to review unexplained outgoing activity and advise the supported account-security steps. It is not a request for staff to prove who is guilty before helping. Do not include passwords or sign-in codes. If a recognised support process needs identity verification, follow its official instructions under the account owner's control, with trusted assistance where appropriate.\n\n**Prioritise continuing harm.** A suspicious school notice that nobody acted on may allow time for routine verification. Unexpected account messages may require prompt account-owner and school-support action. A possible fraudulent payment should be reported promptly to the relevant payment provider. An immediate personal-safety threat needs urgent trusted safeguarding or local emergency assistance; do not delay for a perfect cybersecurity report. This lesson does not supply a universal emergency number or assume one service is appropriate everywhere. The learner should use established local arrangements and trusted adults as needed.\n\n**Handle an unavailable channel.** Record when the first contact was attempted, check that it was the genuine route and use an established alternate when the consequence warrants it. Sade can seek her known teacher's help in reaching authorised school support, rather than trusting the new code-collecting chat. If provider-supported account protection is available, she can use it without waiting for a reply, while recording the action. Avoid sending the same private evidence to a growing list of unverified people. A consistent incident note helps each legitimate helper understand the situation.\n\n**Routing exercise.** Choose a recipient and first request for three supplied cases: messages the school-account owner denies sending; a confirmed transfer to a recipient later found to be unverified; and a social-media threat naming a student's usual route home. For each, explain what should happen if the normal contact is unavailable. Then write a follow-up for Sade adding that a second message was sent after her first report, without changing the original report's times.\n\n**Explained answer.** The school-account case goes through known institutional support, with a trusted teacher or adult helping reach an authorised alternate. The transfer case needs the payment provider's established fraud or dispute route promptly; school staff can support the student but cannot promise recovery. The threat requires urgent safeguarding attention and appropriate local emergency help if danger is immediate, rather than waiting for account analysis. Sade's follow-up refers to the original report, adds the new observation and asks for the next safe action. It should not accuse a classmate based on a display name.\n\n**Reflection.** What would a helpful acknowledgement tell the reporter? It could confirm receipt, give a reference, explain safe interim actions and say when another update is expected. The learner should not demand private information about other accounts. Effective escalation creates a clear next owner and keeps the reporter informed without transferring secrets or encouraging amateur investigation."
    },
    {
      "position": 8,
      "title": "Writing a clear case conclusion",
      "content": "**A conclusion connects evidence to a practical decision.** This final lesson develops a short case report that someone else can understand and challenge. You will state the original question, identify the evidence that resolved it, name the limits and recommend a proportionate next action. The earlier lessons supplied recording, comparison, source tracing, event interpretation and safe escalation skills. Now use them together. A longer report is not necessarily stronger; the important requirement is that each conclusion has support.\n\nWorked example: A fictional school reading-club notice says the meeting has moved from Tuesday to Thursday and asks students to upload identity documents to a new form. A saved club page still lists Tuesday. Learners report the notice through the known school office without using the new form. The office confirms that the meeting really moved to Thursday but that no identity-document collection was authorised. The same notice therefore contains one confirmed change and one unsupported demand. A simple “true” or “fake” label would hide the useful distinction.\n\n**Use a five-part report.** First, state the question: whether the new date and document request were authorised. Second, list the small number of key observations, including the source and approximate time. Third, explain the independent check and its result. Fourth, state what remains unknown. Fifth, give the action for the intended reader. Do not include private learner details or the new form's live address in a class summary. Staff can request necessary original evidence through the appropriate route.\n\n**Worked conclusion.** “The school office, contacted through the existing school directory, confirmed the Thursday meeting and denied authorising the document request. The saved club page had not yet been updated. Learners should use the recognised school announcement route for the current schedule and should not submit identity documents to the unconfirmed form. We did not determine who created or forwarded the combined notice.” This tells classmates what to do while avoiding unsupported claims about the sender. It also explains why the old official page was not enough to settle the date.\n\n**Include actions already taken.** Suppose one learner entered a name into the form but uploaded no document. Record that exposure accurately in a private support note and seek the responsible school's or provider's advice as appropriate. Do not change “entered a name” into “identity stolen,” and do not omit it because the main notice has now been checked. A conclusion can resolve a claim while leaving a separate support task open. Give that task an owner or established contact rather than writing “someone should investigate.”\n\n**Challenge the wording.** Consider three proposed sentences: “Everyone who forwarded it was a scammer”; “The meeting change was false”; and “The office did not authorise the document request.” The first invents intention, and the second contradicts the supplied confirmation. The third is supported at its stated scope. Add the source and time so a later reader can judge whether the guidance is still current. If new evidence changes the instruction, append a correction and explain what changed instead of pretending the earlier uncertainty never existed.\n\n**Final exercise.** Write a report of no more than two short paragraphs for the reading-club case, followed by three bullet points identifying a verified result, an unresolved question and a learning improvement. Then ask a peer to locate the source for every factual claim. Provide an alternative ending in which the office cannot be reached and the saved page is the only independent source. Decide what the report can honestly conclude and what protective boundary should remain.\n\n**Explained answer.** In the completed case, Thursday and the unauthorised document demand are separate findings. The creator's identity remains unknown; updating the club page is an appropriate improvement for the responsible office. In the unavailable-office ending, the new date remains unconfirmed and the unexpected document demand should not be acted on merely because it is urgent. State the source conflict, seek a known organiser or trusted adult and keep the existing facts intact. An unresolved inquiry can still produce a safe interim decision.\n\n**Reflection and further study.** Which claim in your report would change if the office later issued a genuine document requirement through its normal process? Verification must apply to that new instruction rather than treating this case as a permanent rule about every form.\n\n**Further reading — checked 31 October 2026.** UK NCSC, Phishing scams: how to spot and report them: https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/phishing-scams — accessible official guidance on suspicious communications; its UK reporting services do not replace a Nigerian learner's school, provider or local reporting arrangements. Google Account Help, See devices with account access: https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/3067630?hl=en — accessible provider guidance explaining Google devices and sessions; other services have different controls. Both pages were opened and their relevance checked. They are optional reading, not a requirement to use either service or proof that this course has independent editorial approval."
    }
  ]
}
